    <section class="section-pad" id="courses" aria-labelledby="course-heading">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="text-center mb-5 fade-up">
                <div class="eyebrow">CA Foundation Program </div>
                <h2 class="section-title" id="course-heading">Everything You Need to Know About <span class="text-gold">CA Foundation</span></h2>
                <div class="divider-gold"></div>
                <p class="section-subtitle">A complete guide to the CA Foundation course — eligibility, exam pattern, subjects, and preparation strategy — curated by our expert faculty.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="row g-5 align-items-start">
                <div class="col-lg-7 fade-up">
                    <h3 class="mb-3">What is CA Foundation?</h3>
                    <p>CA Foundation is the entry-level examination of the Chartered Accountancy course, conducted by the <strong>Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI)</strong>. It serves as the gateway to one of India's most prestigious professional qualifications. Students who successfully clear CA Foundation, followed by CA Intermediate and CA Final, earn the right to use the title <em>Chartered Accountant (CA)</em>.</p>
                    <p>The CA Foundation examination is held twice a year — in <strong>May/June</strong> and <strong>November/December</strong>. With the right coaching, dedication, and strategy, Class 12 students from Commerce, Science, or Arts streams can clear this exam in their very first attempt.</p>

                    <h3 class="mt-5 mb-3">Eligibility for CA Foundation 2025</h3>
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table icai-table rounded overflow-hidden">
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Requirement</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td class="feature-name">Minimum Qualification</td><td>Class 12 pass (any stream)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="feature-name">Registration Timing</td><td>On or before 1st Jan (for May exam) / 1st July (for Nov exam)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="feature-name">Age Limit</td><td>No minimum or maximum age</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="feature-name">Early Registration</td><td>Can register after Class 10 pass (4 months study period required)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="feature-name">Attempts Per Year</td><td>2 (May and November)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="feature-name">Number of Papers</td><td>4 papers across 2 groups</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3 class="mt-5 mb-3">CA Foundation Exam Pattern</h3>
                    <p>The CA Foundation exam consists of <strong>4 papers</strong>. Papers 1 and 2 are subjective (descriptive), while Papers 3 and 4 are objective (MCQ-based). The exam is conducted over two days.</p>
                    <div class="table-responsive mb-4">
                        <table class="table icai-table rounded overflow-hidden">
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Paper</th><th>Subject</th><th>Marks</th><th>Type</th><th>Duration</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Paper 1</td><td>Principles & Practice of Accounting</td><td>100</td><td>Subjective</td><td>3 hrs</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Paper 2</td><td>Business Laws</td><td>100</td><td>Subjective</td><td>3 hrs</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Paper 3</td><td>Quantitative Aptitude</td><td>100</td><td>MCQ (Negative Marking)</td><td>2 hrs</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Paper 4</td><td>Business Economics</td><td>100</td><td>MCQ (Negative Marking)</td><td>2 hrs</td></tr>
                                <tr><td colspan="2"><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>400</strong></td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3 class="mt-4 mb-3">Passing Criteria</h3>
                    <p>To pass CA Foundation, students must score a minimum of <strong>40% in each paper</strong> AND an aggregate of <strong>50% (200/400 marks)</strong> across all 4 papers. There is no group exemption concept in Foundation — all four papers must be attempted together.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="col-lg-5 fade-up">
                    <h3 class="mb-4">CA Foundation Subjects in Detail</h3>
                    <div class="d-flex flex-column gap-3">
                        <div class="subject-card">
                            <div class="subject-code">Paper 1 · Subjective</div>
                            <h5>Principles & Practice of Accounting</h5>
                            <p>The foundation of all financial understanding. Covers journal entries, ledger preparation, trial balance, financial statements, depreciation, partnership accounts, and more. This paper requires both conceptual clarity and practical problem-solving speed.</p>
                            <div class="subject-marks">Marks: <strong>100</strong> | Passing: <strong>40 marks</strong></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="subject-card">
                            <div class="subject-code">Paper 2 · Subjective</div>
                            <h5>Business Laws</h5>
                            <p>Covers the Indian Contract Act, Sale of Goods Act, Negotiable Instruments Act, Limited Liability Partnership Act, and Companies Act basics. Requires careful reading, precise writing, and memory of key sections and landmark cases.</p>
                            <div class="subject-marks">Marks: <strong>100</strong> | Passing: <strong>40 marks</strong></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="subject-card">
                            <div class="subject-code">Paper 3 · MCQ with Negative Marking</div>
                            <h5>Quantitative Aptitude</h5>
                            <p>Divided into Mathematics (Ratio, Indices, Logarithm, Equations, Sets, Functions) and Statistics (Measures of Central Tendency, Dispersion, Probability, Correlation). A high-scoring paper for students with strong analytical skills and consistent practice.</p>
                            <div class="subject-marks">Marks: <strong>100</strong> | Passing: <strong>40 marks</strong> | ¼ mark deducted per wrong answer</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="subject-card">
                            <div class="subject-code">Paper 4 · MCQ with Negative Marking</div>
                            <h5>Business Economics</h5>
                            <p>Covers Demand and Supply, Market Structures, National Income, Money and Banking, and Indian Economy basics. Objective in nature but requires deep conceptual understanding to handle indirect or application-based questions without guessing.</p>
                            <div class="subject-marks">Marks: <strong>100</strong> | Passing: <strong>40 marks</strong> | ¼ mark deducted per wrong answer</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="scholarship-card mt-4">
                        <div class="eyebrow">Career After CA</div>
                        <h4 class="mt-2">Starting Salary After CA: ₹7–25 LPA</h4>
                        <p style="font-size:.9rem">A qualified CA can work as an auditor, tax consultant, CFO, financial analyst, or start their own practice. Top CA firms pay freshers ₹7–12 LPA, while Big 4 firms offer ₹15–25 LPA. Senior CAs and partners earn ₹50 LPA+.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

